  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NIHR DA 1: Co Producing a research agenda on climate change and refugee health</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A low- and middle-income country (LMIC) award led by the American University of Beirut, Lebanon in partnership with Imperial College London, UK. Refugees in the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) region have insecure living conditions. This makes them a vulnerable population to the increasing impacts of climate change through extreme weather events such as heatwaves, droughts, floods and sandstorms. There is a demonstrated need for climate-related vulnerabilities to be considered for refugees and the development of partnerships with academic, policy, community and humanitarian actors are important to co-produce solutions and policies that will reduce the health impact of climate change on refugee populations.

Commencing in January 2025, this one-year award aims to generate a research agenda about climate change and refugee health via the development of a conceptual framework and defining and refining research questions based on existing knowledge and the needs of the community and relevant actors. This award will establish a stakeholder advisory group to understand their key priorities and needs through interactive workshops, and work to identify the needs and priorities of refugees through refugee community group workshops and regular meetings. The main output from this award is the development of a proposal, which is based on established equitable partnerships, for a research programme that addresses the needs and priorities of refugees, decision-makers and humanitarian partners on climate change and refugee health.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The award has four objectives:

1. To establish new partnerships between the American University of Beirut, Imperial College London, and other Middle East and North Africa (MENA) regional partners on climate change and health.
2. To develop a conceptual framework for climate change and refugee health, and to define and refine research questions on climate change and refugee health based on existing knowledge and the needs of the community and actors.
3. To train and strengthen the team in writing research proposals and identified capacity building needs.
4. To design and develop a future research programme on climate change and refugee health that can be submitted for a relevant funding call.</narrative>
  </description>
